semantic_diff: |
  Base: /Users/jinhun/GitHub/pave at a6acc851bd553eb198636000688e715d8bd9173f (main, pave-init 2.6.3), verified clean over sources/ and skills/pave-init/; build_packages.py --check exit 0; stamp_version.py --check exit 0.
  Kind: run_setup. No node, outcome, edge, check, role, or state field of references/pave-init.pave.yaml changes (validate_pave.py PASS: 26 nodes, 71 edges, 9 control nodes, unchanged). The hunks change the lead-stage procedure of assemble_graph_plan and the plan gate (orchestration, SKILL.md), a hook's text, a validator's read scope, and design-guide text (pave-spec) that generated graphs are planned against. assemble_graph_plan's purpose ("run the global trim pass") stays true: fusion and the fresh-eyes cuts are the trim rule applied to node boundaries and to the assembled whole.

  1. Inverse one-agent test at assembly (pave-spec 9.12; explore-and-plan 4.6 step 2 and a 4.7 design check; one clause each in SKILL.md Stage 3 and README). From: the spec asks only whether a node should split, node by node; nothing asks once over the assembled graph whether adjacent nodes should still be separate. To: for every edge on a success outcome, fuse when one bounded goal holds both nodes with six properties intact (externally visible outcomes, user authority, required reviewer separation, a recovery route that branches between them, an irreversible side-effect boundary, bounded-context feasibility); repeat to a fixed point; record per edge the verdict and the one question that decided it. Why it holds for every run: queue planning shows each planner one subtree, so a boundary that stopped earning its place is invisible to every planner and visible only at assembly; without the inverse a graph can only grow. Cost per run: one lead-run pass over the success edges, no seat. Principles: 4.11, 9.12, warning sign "ceremonial decomposition". Grows the spec by 13 lines.

  2. Fresh-eyes simplify readers before the whole-graph review (explore-and-plan 4.6 step 3; reviewer template whole-graph clause; SKILL.md Stage 3 clause). From: the lead's own trim pass, then the material reviewer's whole-graph round. To: after trim and fusion, four one-shot read-only workers, one angle each (simplification, altitude, efficiency, reuse), return cut candidates; the lead rules on each (applied, or rejected with the reason) in build/ working state, rebuilds, repeats the trim to a fixed point, then sends the bundle with the record to the retained reviewer, whose whole-graph round now judges the rulings. Tiering is stated as capability tiers (top for simplification and altitude, strong for efficiency, fast for reuse); the native binding maps tiers to models. Why it holds for every run: the lead assembled the graph from a run-filled context and is its author (4.4, separate creation and challenge); the material reviewer's stance is defect-finding, and the recorded run shows it PASSed a graph that then lost ten state fields, a seat, two scripts, three data files and eight verifier dispatches per run to four single-question readers. Cost per run: four one-shot dispatches, once, outside every loop; the answer is judgment, not knowable from disk, so it is not ceremony under the updater contract's test. Candidate 8 of the brief (multi-resume efficiency defaults) is folded into the efficiency angle's question, not added as a rule: 9.8, 9.10 and 2.1 already state the principle, and the pass is the mechanism that found the instances. Attackable claim: one reader over all four angles would be cheaper; the run's angle lists overlapped on three of 32 candidates, so the angles are near-independent and run in parallel (9.6).

  3. Stop check without an acknowledgement-only reply (hooks/stop_alignment_check.sh; tests/test_hooks.sh). From: "answer only the questions where you find an issue; otherwise reply lgtm". To: answer every question in one line that names the fact behind it (a path, a run-state entry, a seat name, or "none: why"); a pending decision, a working reviewer or a recorded pause are answers, named. Cadence unchanged: one block in three stops; cooldown, stop_hook_active, marker-only discovery and fail-open untouched; 60 hook tests pass, one added (the block text demands one line per question and carries no "lgtm"). Every copy: references/lead-hooks.md has carried the no-acknowledgement form since the 2.6.0 template rewrite ("There is no acknowledgement-only exit"), so it needs no change; the copy that lagged was pave-init's own hook. The only "lgtm" strings left are the hook's header comment explaining the removal and the test's negative grep. The Codex port registers the same script (codex/hooks/hooks.json); its test keeps the "Socratic check" phrase, which stays.

  4. Round counter restarts on a user change request (pave-spec 9.8; SKILL.md Stage 4). From: plan_review_rounds is a total counter; four rounds with a HIGH route repair_blocked, whatever moved the inputs. To: a user change request at the gate (revision_requested) restarts the counter at zero for the bundle that follows, recorded with the request; defect-class recurrence keeps counting. Why it holds: 9.8's bound exists to stop repairs that cannot converge on fixed inputs; a first round over inputs the user just moved is not that. Graph unchanged: the plan-gate bound is prose, and update_review_rounds_remain (the update path) is untouched. Attackable claim: a restart could extend a stuck loop; the trigger is the user's own act and the class count does not reset.

  5. Size the user gate to the decision (pave-spec 8.3). From: nothing says how a user gate over many effects is shaped, so planners default to a gate per action. To: one approval per vetted list of effects (each line: action, change, why, evidence; checked by an actor other than the runner), a mechanical guard on the list, a failed line stops it, only changed lines return. Why it holds for every run: any generated workflow with user-gated irreversible effects faces the choice, and the per-action default is the friction the user removes at the gate itself, reopening the plan. Placed beside 8.3's "not re-asked by shape" rule. Grows the spec by 9 lines.

  6. Enforcement table as rules; run inputs in the package plan (explore-and-plan 4.6 step 6; section 5). From: "merge the node-local entries; deduplicate guards proposed for the same prohibition", which still yields a row per node and per source. To: one row per rule or per dispatched seat (9.14.1's seat records stay); for a rule, the prohibition in the reader's words with what it protects, its level and two reasons, and its source nodes; never a second row for the same rule per node or per source. The run-setup bullet names every run input with its source and the collection rule (one bounded question per missing input, recorded verbatim, never guessed). Replaces the step-6 text; one clause in two section-5 bullets.

  7. Framework words are codenames (pave-spec 8.5; hooks/write_for_reader.sh). From: 8.5 binds identifiers; approval-briefs says define a technical term at first use. To: 8.5 names the framework's own words (seat, gate, node, write) as codenames to a reader outside the run; the reminder hook names them. approval-briefs is unchanged because its rule covers a technical term once 8.5 says these are one. Grows 8.5 by two lines.

  8. Planning-queue validator reads only returned drafts (scripts/validate_run_state.py; tests/test_validate_run_state.py; references/planning-layout.md). From: every dispatched entry's draft is parsed, so a sibling planner's half-written file fails the queue check. To: a pending_dispatched draft is never read; planned, reviewed and stale drafts are read as before. Not a weakening: an in-flight file was never a claim, and the same file is checked the moment the lead marks the entry planned. One test added: the same half-written file passes at pending_dispatched and fails at planned.

  Rejected: no candidate rejected outright; candidate 8 is folded into 2, candidate 10(b) is 1.
  Usage evidence answered: the run's revision record names one defect, the validator race, fixed in 8. The seat evidence for 2 is the recorded run itself: three whole-graph reviewer rounds PASSed structure that four readers then cut. pave-init has no earlier usage record beyond this run's review record.
  Every copy: "trim pass" survivors checked. node-planner.md.tmpl step 6 is the planner's node-local trim (different scope, stays); pave-init-traceability.md's assemble_graph_plan row already reads "Merge, simplify, bind, produce approval bundle" (stays). "every dispatched entry's node draft" replaced in the validator docstring and planning-layout.md; the schema's draft description ("required from pending_dispatched onward") stays true because the path is still required, only the read moved.
  User-only changes check (revision rule 6): root goal and acceptance unchanged; allowed and forbidden effects unchanged; external access unchanged; public interfaces unchanged (the hook still blocks one stop in three; validator exit codes for returned entries unchanged); evidence and check strength: the stop check demands more, not less, and the validator's check on a claim is unchanged; state authority unchanged; pave-init carries no user-approved budget, and the four-round bound restarts only on the user's own act with class recurrence continuing, flagged here as the one point a reviewer may weigh. Result: none. pave-init's release is the user's decision always, so the gate is presented regardless.
  Growth: pave-spec +31 lines/+2009 bytes; explore-and-plan +2 lines/+3078 bytes; SKILL.md +440 bytes; README +3 lines/+234 bytes; planning-layout +166 bytes; reviewer template +318 bytes. Cut: the two lgtm sentences and the step-6 dedupe clause. Ceilings raised in test_doc_budget.py by exactly the growth, dated 2026-09-24 (2.7.0). Generated files (skills/*/SKILL.md, agents/*.md, codex/skills/*/SKILL.md, codex/agents/*.toml) and version stamps (.claude-plugin/plugin.json, .codex-plugin/plugin.json, .claude-plugin/marketplace.json, README version line) are build and stamp products, included so the patch is self-contained: build_packages.py --check and stamp_version.py --check exit 0 on the applied tree. Not hand-edited; delete them and rebuild to confirm.
  Round 1 review (update-reviewer PASS, four LOW): LOW-1 step 6 now reads "one row per rule or per dispatched seat ... never a second row for the same rule per node or per source", in explore-and-plan 4.6, section 5 and the changelog, so 9.14.1's seat records survive the merge. LOW-2 the reviewer template's whole-graph list keeps "binding marking" beside the trim, fusion and simplify rulings; the phrase had been dropped without a reason. LOW-3 the changelog count is the recorded one: 23 rows to eight rules (run-state and skill-package-plan), not the 31 of the superseded proposal. LOW-4 the ceilings line is a tagged top-level entry (Changed). Optional accepted: 4.6 step 2 says a moved guard "stays a precondition of the later member's activity", because a guard read as post-B would let fusion run B on an unchecked A, which breaks the irreversible side-effect property the test preserves.
  Mechanical (scratch worktree at the base): bash skills/pave-init/tests/test_hooks.sh 60 passed 0 failed; python3 -m unittest discover -s skills/pave-init/tests Ran 83 OK (size ceiling test included); python3 -m unittest discover -s codex/tests Ran 51, 1 failure pre-existing at the untouched base (test_release_tools.PreflightProofTests.test_release_mode_forces_v2_slots_and_exact_project_trust, a macOS /var vs /private/var temp-path compare; CI runs Linux).
